---
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
---
<figure class="plate">
  <img src={u("/art/pfullingen-1944.webp")}
       alt="A constructed illustration: a long range of red-brick weaving sheds with sawtooth north-light roofs and a tall chimney in a narrow Swabian valley, half-timbered houses on the wooded slope behind, frost on the ground, and a line of men in worn civilian clothes walking to the shift along a cinder path beside a canalised stream, all seen from behind."
       width="1700" height="886" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
  <figcaption class="cap" style="margin-top:12px">
    <strong>Pfullingen, 1944.</strong> A constructed illustration, made for this archive by an image model working from a
    written brief. It is illustration and not evidence, and nobody in it has a face.
    <br /><br />
    It is drawn as a works and not as a camp, because that is what the papers describe: foreign labour in a Württemberg
    textile town, billeted and walked to the shift. There is no wire in it, no guard, no insignia and no flag — not to
    soften anything, but because putting in what the record does not say is how a picture starts lying. What the record
    does hold is the list of foreigners at Pfullingen in 1947, certified by that town's mayor in 1950, and the Arolsen
    papers behind it.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
<style>
  .plate { margin: 0; }
  .plate img { display: block; width: 100%; max-width: 820px; height: auto; border-radius: 2px; }
</style>
